128: ## 8. Business rules
129: 
130: | ID | Rule | Rationale |
131: |---|---|---|
132: | BR-001 | All members of a company can view all of its notes, versions, action items, and transcripts; nobody 
outside the company can. | Notes are company memory (r1 q4). |
133: | BR-002 | Any member can create a note in any space of their company, including private spaces. | Notes can be 
written after the meeting (r2 q11). |
134: | BR-003 | Edit rights: author, current participants, company admins. Delete rights: author, company admins. | r1 
q5, r2 q11. |
135: | BR-004 | Action items: note editors create/edit/delete; editors and the assignee toggle completion. | The 
assignee closes their own work (r2 q8). |
136: | BR-005 | Participants and assignees must be active members of the note's company. | Prevents cross-company 
references. |
137: | BR-006 | AI suggestions never become assignments or stored content without explicit user confirmation. | Avoids 
wrong attributions (r1 q12). |
138: | BR-007 | Each save creates one immutable version; restore creates a new version; action items are current-state 
only. | r1 q8, r2 q10. |
139: | BR-008 | The daily limit applies to every company, counts only successful drafts (including reprocessing), and 
is adjustable by admins from 1 to 200. | Cost and abuse protection (r1 q11, r2 q4). |
140: | BR-009 | Only admins configure AI; the API key is write-only from the browser's perspective and is stored 
encrypted on the server. | r2 q1, q3. |
141: | BR-010 | One transcript per note; a new upload replaces the stored text; the stored transcript is visible to 
every note viewer. | r1 q13, r2 q12. |
142: | BR-011 | A save based on an outdated version is rejected. | No silent overwrite (r1 q18). |
143: | BR-012 | AI access uses API keys only; no subscription credentials are requested, stored, or proxied. | 
Anthropic policy; OpenAI sign-in not available (r3). |
144: 
145: ## 9. Data and integrations
146: 
147: ### Data inputs
148: 
149: - Note fields, participants, rich-text content, action items, transcript files (.txt/.vtt/.srt, UTF-8 text, ≤1 
MB).
150: - Admin AI settings: provider, API key, model, reasoning level, daily limit.
151: 
152: ### Data outputs
153: 
154: - Stored notes, versions, action items, transcripts, AI settings (key encrypted).
155: - AI drafts (transient until saved).
156: - Search results with excerpts; downloaded .md files.
157: 
158: ### Integrations
159: 
160: - Anthropic Messages API (official SDK), OpenAI API (official SDK), Kimi (Moonshot) and GLM (Zhipu/Z.ai) through 
their OpenAI-compatible endpoints. All calls are server-side from authenticated API routes.
161: - Supabase Postgres (RLS, full-text search), Supabase Auth.
162: - The existing `meeting_notes` and `meeting_note_action_items` tables and `IMeetingNoteRepository` are reused and 
extended (for example, `summary` becomes optional for manual notes, and `room_id` no longer cascades on space 
deletion).
163: 
164: ## 10. Constraints and assumptions
165: 
166: ### Constraints
167: 
168: - Next.js App Router, TypeScript strict, Supabase Postgres/Auth/RLS, TanStack Query, shadcn/ui, following 
AGENTS.md (server client in routes, `auth.getUser()`, `users.id` vs `users.supabase_uid`, roles `admin|member`).
169: - API keys never reach client code, logs, error messages, or reports; transcript text is not logged.
170: - AI calls only from the server; the browser never calls a provider.
171: - Size limits: title ≤200 chars; content ≤100,000 chars; ≤50 participants; ≤100 action items per note; transcript 
≤1 MB.
172: - UI in the app's current language (English); AI summary in the transcript's language.
173: - Migrations are written and applied locally during the phase; they are applied to the online database 
`vhabpcoyypobgasacsko` only at phase end with the owner's explicit authorization; rollout order is migrations then 
app, rollback is app first.
174: - Only integration and E2E tests (no unit tests); one test process at a time; one final Chrome-only E2E run.
175: 
176: ### Assumptions
177: 
178: - The owner creates an OpenAI platform API key (billed separately from ChatGPT Plus/Pro) for the final test 
against the real provider (confirmed r3).
179: - Automated tests use a local fake AI server compatible with the provider APIs (confirmed r2 q17).
180: 
181: ## 11. Edge cases and failure modes
182: 
183: | Case | Expected behavior |
184: |---|---|
185: | Two editors save the same note concurrently | The later save is rejected with a conflict warning showing the 
newer version (FR-007). |
186: | Empty / >1 MB / wrong extension / binary file | Rejected before AI, clear message, nothing saved, not counted 
(FR-013). |
187: | .vtt/.srt with cue numbers and timestamps | Stripped; speaker names kept (FR-014). |
188: | Provider error, timeout, refusal, invalid key | Note and transcript stay saved; error + "Try again"; not 
counted (FR-020). |
189: | User leaves the page while processing | Draft discarded; not counted (FR-021). |
190: | AI suggests a name matching no member or several members | Item left unassigned for the user to choose 
(FR-017). |
191: | Daily limit reached | Processing refused with limit and reset time (FR-024). |
192: | No AI configured | Manual notes work; AI disabled with message; admin sees settings link (FR-023). |
193: | Space deleted | Notes kept, labelled "Space removed" (FR-027). |
194: | Participant/assignee leaves the company | Shown as "Former member"; no access (FR-028). |
195: | Assignee is not a note editor | Can toggle completion of their item only (BR-004). |
196: | Restore of an old version | New version created; action items unchanged (FR-009). |
197: | Transcript contains instructions aimed at the AI | Treated as data; output limited to the draft fields; 
suggested assignees resolved only against the company member list. |
198: | Pasted content with scripts or `javascript:` links | Removed by sanitization (FR-002). |
199: | Member of company A requests a note, version, item, search, or setting of company B | Not found / denied 
(BR-001, SM-3). |
200: | Non-admin calls the AI settings route | Denied (BR-009). |
201: 
202: ## 12. Security, privacy, compliance, and abuse considerations
203: 
204: - RLS on every new or changed table, comparing `users.supabase_uid` with `auth.uid()::text` and scoping by 
company; API routes resolve the application user by `supabase_uid`.
205: - API keys stored encrypted at rest, readable only by server code, never returned (only a mask), never logged.
206: - Rich text sanitized on write and render; Markdown export escapes content so it cannot inject HTML when rendered 
elsewhere.
207: - Transcript text is sent to a third-party provider only after the notice naming that provider; the admin's 
provider choice (including providers hosted outside Brazil/US) is the company's decision.
208: - Prompt-injection resistance: transcript is passed as data; output parsed against a fixed schema; any field 
outside the schema is discarded.
209: - Abuse: daily limit per company; size limits; server-side validation of every limit.
210: - Subscription credentials (Claude, ChatGPT) are never requested or stored.
211: 
212: ## 13. Accessibility, localization, and usability considerations
213: 
214: - Editor, version list, review step, and dialogs are keyboard-operable with visible focus and labelled controls 
(Radix/shadcn primitives).
215: - Clear progress state while processing; errors state what happened and what to do.
216: - UI strings in English, matching the app; summary language follows the transcript.
217: - Interactive elements inside clickable cards follow the AGENTS.md interaction rules.
