1: # SPEC: Phase 6 — Meeting Notes
2: 
3: Goal: Company members can record, revise, search, and export meeting notes per space — with action items, version 
history, and an AI-generated draft summary and action items from an uploaded transcript using the company's own AI 
provider key.
4: 
5: ## 1. Summary
6: 
7: Virtual Office gains a meeting-notes capability. A member writes a note for a
8: space (title, meeting date, participants, rich-text content), tracks action
9: items, and can revise, restore, delete, search, and export notes. A member can
10: upload a meeting transcript (.txt, .vtt, .srt); the server sends it to the AI
11: provider configured by the company admin (Anthropic Claude, OpenAI, Kimi, or
12: GLM, using the company's own API key) and returns a draft summary and
13: suggested action items that the member reviews before anything is saved.
14: This delivers roadmap requirements MEET-01..MEET-07 plus Markdown export.
15: MEET-08 (assignment notifications and due-date reminders) moves to Phase 7.
16: 
17: ## 2. Problem statement
18: 
19: Meetings happen in Virtual Office spaces, but nothing they decide is kept in
20: the product: there is no way to write notes, track who owes what, or find a
21: past decision. The database already has `meeting_notes` and
22: `meeting_note_action_items` tables and an unused repository, but users have no
23: write path and no UI. The dashboard shows an "AI Meeting Notes" card that leads
24: nowhere. Teams lose decisions and follow-ups, and the product cannot show its
25: AI meeting-notes promise to investors.
26: 
27: ## 3. Goals and success metrics
28: 
29: ### Goals
30: 
31: - G1: Members can use meeting notes in real daily work, at production quality.
32: - G2: The transcript-to-draft AI flow is polished enough to demonstrate to investors.
33: - G3: Each company uses and pays for AI with its own provider key; the platform never holds subscription 
credentials.
34: 
35: ### Success metrics
36: 
37: - SM-1: A one-hour transcript (≤1 MB) produces a reviewable draft within 60 seconds with the configured real 
provider.
38: - SM-2: Search returns the first page within 1 second with 1,000 notes in the company.
39: - SM-3: Zero notes, versions, action items, transcripts, or AI settings of another company are ever visible or 
modifiable.
40: 
41: ## 4. Users and stakeholders
42: 
43: ### Primary users
44: 
45: - Company member: creates notes, edits notes they authored or participate in, completes action items assigned to 
them, uploads transcripts, searches, exports.
46: - Company admin (`role = admin`): everything a member can do, plus edits and deletes any note of the company and 
configures the company's AI provider, key, model, reasoning level, and daily limit.
47: 
48: ### Secondary users
49: 
50: - Participant: a member listed on a note; may edit it.
51: - Assignee: a member assigned an action item; may mark it complete.
52: 
53: ### Stakeholders
54: 
55: - Product owner (Giuliano): runs acceptance against the online database; demonstrates the feature to investors.
56: 
57: ## 5. Scope
58: 
59: ### In scope
60: 
61: - Notes per space: create, view, edit, delete; rich-text content; participants.
62: - Version history with view and restore; concurrent-edit conflict detection.
63: - Action items with assignee, due date, and completion.
64: - Transcript upload (.txt, .vtt, .srt), AI draft summary and suggested action items, review-before-save, reprocess.
65: - Company AI settings (admin): provider among Anthropic, OpenAI, Kimi, GLM; API key; model and reasoning level; 
daily limit.
66: - Full-text search with space and period filters.
67: - Markdown (.md) export of a note.
68: - "Notes" tab in the space detail and a "Meetings" page; the dashboard "AI Meeting Notes" card links to the 
Meetings page.
69: - Notes survive deletion of their space.
70: 
71: ### Out of scope
72: 
73: - MEET-08 assignment notifications and due-date reminders (moved to Phase 7, Announcements, which builds the 
shared notification center and scheduler).
74: - Sign-in with a ChatGPT/Codex or Claude subscription (Anthropic prohibits it for third-party apps; OpenAI's "Sign 
in with ChatGPT" is a partner-only preview). Future item, contingent on OpenAI granting access.
75: - PDF export, note templates, calendar integration, sharing notes outside the company.
76: - Automatic recording or transcription of in-app audio calls.
77: - Real-time collaborative editing.
78: - Background AI processing that survives leaving the page.
79: - Semantic (embedding) search; searching inside transcript text.
80: - Per-user model choice (admin-only setting).
81: 
82: ## 6. User journeys
83: 
84: - J1 Manual note: member opens a space's Notes tab → "New note" → enters title, date, participants, content → 
saves → note appears in the tab and on the Meetings page.
85: - J2 Action items: an editor adds items with description, optional assignee and due date; the assignee or an 
editor marks an item complete.
86: - J3 Revise: an editor edits and saves; a new version is recorded. If someone saved in between, the editor sees a 
conflict warning and the newer content before deciding.
87: - J4 History: a viewer opens the version list (author, time), opens an old version; an editor restores it, 
creating a new version.
88: - J5 AI from transcript: member chooses "New note from transcript" (or "Attach transcript" on an existing note) → 
selects a file → sees the notice naming the AI provider → starts processing → reviews and edits the draft summary and 
each suggested action item (accept, edit, reassign, reject) → saves.
89: - J6 Reprocess: an editor reprocesses the stored transcript, obtaining a new draft to review; the current summary 
changes only when the new draft is saved.
90: - J7 Admin setup: admin opens company settings → AI → selects provider, enters API key, selects model and 
reasoning level, sets daily limit → saves; the key is then shown only masked.
91: - J8 Search: member opens Meetings → types words, optionally filters by space and period → sees ranked results 
with matched excerpts → opens a note.
92: - J9 Export: viewer clicks "Export Markdown", optionally ticks "Include transcript", and downloads a .md file.
93: - J10 Delete: author or admin deletes a note after confirmation.
94: 
219: ## 14. Acceptance criteria
220: 
221: - AC-001 (FR-001, FR-003, FR-005, BR-002): Given a member of company A, when they create a note in a space with 
title, date, two participants, and formatted content, then it appears first in that space's Notes tab and opens 
showing all fields.
222: - AC-002 (FR-001, BR-005): Given a title of 201 chars, content above 100,000 chars, 51 participants, or a 
participant from another company, when saving, then the save is rejected with a message and nothing is stored.
223: - AC-003 (FR-002): Given content containing a script tag and a `javascript:` link, when saved and viewed, then no 
script runs and the link is removed or neutralized.
224: - AC-004 (FR-006, BR-003): Given a note authored by user X with participant Y, when Y and an admin edit it, then 
both saves succeed; when a non-participant member Z tries to edit (UI or direct API call), then it is denied and Z 
sees no edit control.
225: - AC-005 (FR-007, BR-011): Given two sessions open the same note version, when both save different content, then 
the first succeeds and the second gets a conflict warning showing the first's content, and the first's content is not 
overwritten.
226: - AC-006 (FR-008, FR-009, BR-007): Given a note saved three times, when a viewer opens history, then three 
versions show author and time; when an editor restores version 1, then a fourth version equals version 1's fields and 
the action items are unchanged.
227: - AC-007 (FR-010, BR-003): Given a note with versions, items, and a transcript, when the author confirms delete, 
then the note, versions, items, and transcript are gone; when a participant who is not the author or an admin tries to 
delete, then it is denied.
228: - AC-008 (FR-011, BR-004): Given a note editor, when they add an item with description, assignee, and due date, 
then it is shown; when the assignee (not an editor) toggles completion, then it succeeds; when the assignee tries to 
edit the description or delete, then it is denied.
229: - AC-009 (FR-011): Given a note with 100 items, when adding a 101st or an item with an empty description, then it 
is rejected.
230: - AC-010 (FR-012, FR-015, FR-016, FR-018, FR-019, BR-006, BR-010): Given a configured provider (fake AI server in 
automated tests), when a member uploads a valid .txt to create a note, then a notice names the provider, a draft with 
summary and suggested items appears, and nothing is stored until save; after editing the summary, rejecting one item 
and accepting another, saving stores exactly the edited summary, the accepted item, and the transcript; reprocessing 
returns a new draft and leaves the stored summary unchanged until saved; attaching a transcript to an existing note 
replaces the previous one.
231: - AC-011 (FR-013): Given an empty file, a 1.1 MB file, a .pdf renamed .txt, or a .docx, when uploading, then each 
is rejected with a message, no AI call is made, nothing is stored, and the daily count is unchanged.
232: - AC-012 (FR-014): Given a .vtt and a .srt with cue numbers, timestamps, and speaker names, when processed, then 
the text sent to the provider contains no cue numbers or timestamps and keeps the speaker names.
233: - AC-013 (FR-017, BR-006): Given a draft suggesting "Ana" when one active member is Ana, "Bruno" when two members 
match, and "Carla" when none does, then Ana is pre-linked and Bruno's and Carla's items are unassigned.
234: - AC-014 (FR-004, FR-025, BR-001, SM-2, SM-3): Given 1,000 notes in company A and notes in company B containing 
the same words, when a member of A searches an accented word without the accent, with space and period filters, then 
only matching notes of A within the filters are returned, ranked, with excerpts, and the first page returns within 1 
second locally; company B's notes never appear.
235: - AC-015 (FR-004): Given the dashboard, when the member clicks "AI Meeting Notes", then the Meetings page opens.
236: - AC-016 (FR-020, FR-021, FR-024, BR-008): Given the provider returns an error, times out, refuses, or rejects 
the key, then the note and transcript stay saved, an error with "Try again" appears, and the daily count is unchanged; 
given the user navigates away mid-processing, then no draft is stored and the count is unchanged; given the company 
reached its limit, then processing is refused with the limit and reset time.
237: - AC-017 (FR-022, BR-009): Given an admin, when they save provider, key, model, reasoning level, and limit 50, 
then the settings persist, the key is shown only masked, and no API response or page ever contains the key; given a 
member, when calling the settings route, then it is denied; given limit 0 or 201, then it is rejected.
238: - AC-018 (FR-023): Given no provider configured, when a member opens a note, then manual editing works and AI 
controls are disabled with "An admin needs to configure AI"; an admin additionally sees a link to settings.
239: - AC-019 (FR-005, FR-026): Given a note with formatted content, summary, items, and transcript, when exported 
without the option, then the .md contains title, date, space, participants, Markdown content, summary, and items but 
no transcript; with the option ticked, the transcript is included.
240: - AC-020 (FR-027): Given a space with notes, when the space is deleted, then its notes remain on the Meetings 
page and in search, labelled "Space removed".
241: - AC-021 (FR-028): Given a participant and assignee who leaves the company, then the note shows them as "Former 
member" and they can no longer read the note.
242: - AC-022 (SM-1, BR-012): Given the owner's real OpenAI API key configured on the deployed or locally run app 
against the online database, when processing a one-hour fictional transcript, then a draft appears within 60 seconds; 
at no point is a subscription login requested.
243: - AC-023 (SM-3, BR-001, BR-009): Given two companies, when a member of each attempts direct API and database 
access to the other's notes, versions, items, transcripts, search, and AI settings, then every attempt returns nothing 
or is denied.
244: 
245: ## 15. Test strategy
246: 
247: - Levels: integration and E2E only (AGENTS.md). No unit tests; no mocking of Supabase, repositories, or the code 
under test.
248: - Integration (Vitest against local Supabase): RLS and route authorization per role and company (AC-004, AC-007, 
AC-008, AC-017, AC-021, AC-023); validation limits (AC-002, AC-009, AC-011); version creation and conflict (AC-005, 
AC-006); transcript normalization (AC-012); assignee matching (AC-013); limit counting and failure non-counting 
(AC-016); search correctness, isolation, and timing with 1,000 seeded notes (AC-014); space deletion (AC-020); key 
never returned (AC-017).
249: - External AI provider: replaced in automated tests only, by a local fake server speaking the provider APIs, 
selected by environment configuration, with fixed responses and scripted failures (error, timeout, refusal, invalid 
key). Database, RLS, routes, and UI stay real.
250: - E2E (Playwright, two accounts): manual note lifecycle, editing rights, conflict, history/restore, action items 
with assignee completion, transcript → review → save, AI-disabled state, admin settings, search, export download, 
sanitization (AC-001, AC-003–AC-008, AC-010, AC-015, AC-018, AC-019).
251: - Test data: fictional transcripts in .txt/.vtt/.srt, Portuguese and English, including speaker names, accents, 
and malformed files. No real personal data.
252: - Cadence: per task type-check + lint; per block type-check, lint, DB integration suite when server code changed; 
one final Chrome-only E2E run of all new specs plus the full regression suite, zero retries; one test process at a 
time.
253: - Owner test: AC-022 with a real provider key against the online database after the migrations are applied.
254: 
255: ## 16. Validation and launch checklist
256: 
257: - All Must FRs and AC-001..AC-023 have evidence in TRACK.
258: - Adversarial reviews passed at SPEC, PLAN, each block, and final.
259: - Type-check, lint, build, DB integration suite, final E2E run green.
260: - Migrations listed with state: written locally, applied locally, applied to `vhabpcoyypobgasacsko` (after 
authorization, catalog read back, smoke check), app deployed.
261: - ROADMAP updated: Phase 6 status; MEET-08 moved to Phase 7.
262: - Owner acceptance test (AC-022) passed.
263: 
264: ## 17. Open questions
265: 
266: Non-blocking, resolved during planning:
267: 
268: - Exact curated model and reasoning-level list per provider.
269: - Rich-text editor library and stored format.
270: - Key encryption mechanism (for example, Supabase Vault or application-level encryption with a server-only 
secret).
271: - Day boundary for the daily limit (default proposal: UTC calendar day, shown to the user in local time).

